\begin{abstract}
Accurate congestion detection is essential for maintaining Quality of Experience (QoE) in bandwidth-intensive applications. Conventional approaches rely on infrastructure-level metrics and protocol dynamics, often overlooking user-centric indicators of effective capacity. This paper introduces a hybrid framework for congestion detection and \textit{maximum achievable throughput} estimation, combining a lightweight heuristic with a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model enhanced by Elastic Weight Consolidation (EWC). The heuristic detects sustained throughput plateaus by contrasting short- and long-term maxima, distinguishing demand-limited variability from congestion-induced saturation, while the LSTM-EWC model provides adaptive learning across diverse traffic patterns. Evaluation with Mininet and D-ITG under controlled bandwidth constraints shows that the hybrid framework achieves 92.1% accuracy and an 81.5% F1-score, outperforming baseline machine learning models by 13 F1 points. Although the heuristic alone reaches slightly higher accuracy (93.0%), the LSTM-EWC component ensures superior precision–recall balance under heterogeneous conditions. By identifying persistent throughput ceilings caused by network congestion, the framework enables practical estimation of maximum achievable throughput, supporting QoE management, service optimization, and user decisions such as upgrading to higher-tier plans.
\end{abstract}



