\section{Introduction}

Network congestion remains a critical challenge for bandwidth-intensive applications such as video streaming, cloud gaming, and real-time communication. Ensuring high Quality of Experience (QoE) requires timely and accurate congestion detection. Conventional methods, including Active Queue Management (AQM) \cite{floyd1993random, nichols2018codel, athuraliya2001rem} and delay-based approaches such as TCP Vegas \cite{brakmo1994tcp}, operate at the infrastructure level and emphasize fairness and global performance, but often fail to reveal persistent user-level capacity constraints.

A key underexplored aspect is the estimation of a user’s \textit{maximum achievable throughput} - the effective upper bound under current network conditions. For example, a subscriber on a 50 Mbps plan may consistently observe only 40 Mbps throughput due to limited application demand or sustained contention on the access link. Unlike transient losses or delays, such sustained throughput plateaus provide stronger evidence of user-level bandwidth exhaustion and are highly relevant for QoE management and capacity planning.

Recent congestion-control advances, including deep learning-based schemes such as Aurora \cite{jay2019deep}, PCC Vivace \cite{dong2018pcc}, and Sage \cite{10.1145/3603269.3604838}, primarily optimize sending rates and transport behavior. Similarly, methods like Sprout \cite{winstein2013stochastic}, reinforcement learning-based frameworks \cite{10621288}, and CCP \cite{narayan2018restructuring} adapt to dynamic conditions but do not explicitly detect user-level saturation. While reinforcement learning-based congestion control shows promise, systematic evaluations reveal that such approaches often under-perform when bandwidth and latency vary dynamically \cite{10621288}. These methods enhance responsiveness yet fall short in distinguishing between demand-limited and congestion-limited throughput.

To address this gap, we propose a hybrid framework that combines heuristic interpretability with data-driven adaptability. A lightweight heuristic algorithm detects sustained throughput plateaus, while a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model enhanced with Elastic Weight Consolidation (EWC) adapts across traffic patterns without catastrophic forgetting. This unified design leverages both interpretability and generalization, with the potential to remain effective under dynamic bandwidth and latency conditions.

The main contributions of this work are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We formalize \textit{maximum achievable throughput} as sustained throughput plateaus reflecting user-level capacity constraints, distinct from transient congestion events.
\item We develop a hybrid framework that integrates heuristic plateau detection with an EWC-enhanced LSTM for robust learning across diverse traffic scenarios.
\item We validate the framework using Mininet and D-ITG simulations, demonstrating improved accuracy and robustness compared to baseline models.
\end{itemize}



\section{Methodology}

To address the challenge of distinguishing sustained capacity limitations from transient traffic fluctuations, we develop three complementary detection methods. Each method targets a different aspect of user-level congestion, ranging from interpretable heuristic logic to deep learning, with a hybrid strategy that unifies both approaches. The central objective is to identify sustained throughput plateaus that indicate effective maximum achievable throughput, even when these values fall below the nominal capacity of the subscribed network plan.

\subsection{Heuristic Peak Detection Algorithm}

Conventional congestion metrics often misinterpret application-level bursts as network-induced capacity constraints. To address this limitation, we introduce a heuristic method that detects sustained throughput plateaus, which arise when user demand persistently approaches the maximum achievable throughput of the link.

The method is based on a key observation: capacity-limited traffic produces consistent clustering of throughput values near the achievable upper bound, in contrast to the naturally fluctuating peaks and valleys of application-limited behavior. By quantifying the density of near-peak samples over time, the algorithm can reliably differentiate between these conditions with high interpretability.

The detection process involves three sequential steps:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Long-term maximum calculation}: Establish a reference capacity by identifying the maximum throughput within configurable long-duration windows (see Fig.~\ref{fig}).
\item \textbf{Short-term bin analysis}: Within each long window, count the number of samples that fall within a tolerance threshold of the long-term maximum, using short-duration bins (Fig.~\ref{fig}).
\item \textbf{Binary classification}: Label a bin as ``congested" if the near-peak count exceeds a predefined threshold, indicating capacity saturation (Fig.~\ref{fig}).
\end{enumerate}

The full procedure is presented in Algorithm~\ref{alg}, which formalizes how throughput samples are processed and classified. The key configuration parameters controlling the detection logic-such as window durations, peak tolerances, and thresholds-are summarized in Table~\ref{tab}.

