To support continual learning across diverse traffic types, we apply \textit{Elastic Weight Consolidation (EWC)} \cite{doi:10.1073/pnas.1611835114}, which mitigates catastrophic forgetting by penalizing changes to parameters critical for past tasks, identified via the Fisher Information Matrix. The loss is defined as $\mathcal{L}{EWC} = \mathcal{L}{new} + \lambda \sum_{i} F_i (\theta_i - \theta_i^*)^2$, where $F_i$ denotes the Fisher information for parameter $i$ and $\lambda$ controls regularization strength. EWC fine-tuning is operationally efficient, enabling adaptation to new network conditions using only recent data rather than retraining on full historical datasets. This allows incremental updates to pre-trained models while preserving prior knowledge, reducing computational overhead and deployment time, and supporting rapid adaptation under constrained resources.

We evaluate two variants: an EWC-regularized LSTM model fine-tuned sequentially on different traffic test cases, and a baseline LSTM model trained jointly on all data. This comparison demonstrates how EWC enables practical model adaptation while preserving learned knowledge across diverse network environments.

\subsection{Hybrid Model with Parameter Optimization}
