As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig}(a) and (b), the LSTM model effectively captures temporal patterns in buffer occupancy. Sustained high occupancy in the ground truth corresponds to elevated confidence in the model output, reflecting its ability to generalize the relationship between traffic build-up and congestion. The confidence levels increase gradually as buffer load rises and decrease as congestion subsides, indicating a time-aware interpretation of network dynamics.

The comparative analysis in Fig.~\ref{fig}(c) highlights the differing behaviors of heuristic and LSTM-based congestion detection. The heuristic method applies a conservative strategy, identifying congestion primarily when the network operates close to its capacity limit. While this minimizes false alarms, it overlooks periods where bursty traffic causes congestion below the nominal limit. The LSTM model, by contrast, provides more balanced detection, capturing a larger portion of congestion events while maintaining reliable precision, resulting in closer agreement with the ground truth.

This alignment between occupancy trends and model predictions confirms that the hybrid approach leverages the LSTM’s temporal modeling to track buffer evolution with greater fidelity. Overall, the hybrid model demonstrates strong potential for real-time congestion prediction by remaining consistent with observed traffic behavior over time.

